<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      19b-01. `pos.tables.admin` — the permission that decides which tables a restaurant HAS.

      ══ Why not reuse pos.tables.manage ══

      `pos.tables.manage` already exists, and WAITER holds it. That is not an oversight — 055
      granted it deliberately and said why: "Without it a waiter cannot seat a table or attach an
      order to one." Table service does not work if the person carrying the plates cannot mark a
      table occupied.

      Until this phase, that permission gated the only write on TableController: PATCH /{id},
      which sets status. Status is RUNTIME state. This phase adds create / rename / re-capacity /
      retire / reactivate, which is CATALOGUE state, and hanging those off the same code would have
      silently handed every waiter in every tenant the ability to rename and retire the
      restaurant's tables in the middle of a shift. The verbs share a noun and nothing else:

        pos.tables.manage  seat / release a table, attach an order    WAITER CASHIER MANAGER +
        pos.tables.admin   create / rename / re-capacity / retire     MANAGER TENANT_ADMIN OWNER

      ══ Who holds it ══

      OWNER, TENANT_ADMIN, MANAGER. A branch manager already owns the menu (`pos.menu.manage`) and
      the floor is the same kind of decision. CASHIER and WAITER hold NEITHER this nor anything
      that implies it, and pos-service asserts both directions (TableCatalogueIT: a waiter can list
      and seat, and is refused create, update, deactivate, and the includeInactive catalogue view).

      Granted EXPLICITLY, never via a catalogue-wide SELECT. Every blanket grant in this changelog
      (030/041/042/045/046/057) is point-in-time: it enumerates what existed the day it ran and
      retro-grants nothing added afterwards. 055, 059 and 082 all say this and all grant explicitly
      for the same reason. A permission no role holds produces a clean, confident 403 for every
      user including OWNER — the exact defect class PermissionCatalogClosureTest exists to catch,
      and the reason that test scans @PreAuthorize across every service rather than trusting review.
    -->

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-083-pos-tables-admin" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <comment>pos.tables.admin — dining-table catalogue CRUD, kept apart from the waiter-held pos.tables.manage</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
            INSERT INTO permissions (code, module, description) VALUES
              ('pos.tables.admin', 'pos', 'Create, rename, re-capacity, retire and reactivate dining tables (catalogue, not seating)')
            ON CONFLICT (code) DO NOTHING;

            INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code) VALUES
              ('OWNER',        'pos.tables.admin'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN', 'pos.tables.admin'),
              ('MANAGER',      'pos.tables.admin')
            ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
                DELETE FROM role_permissions WHERE permission_code = 'pos.tables.admin';
                DELETE FROM permissions WHERE code = 'pos.tables.admin';
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
      Says so if the grant did not land, rather than leaving a permission that exists and that
      nobody holds — which is invisible at runtime (everyone including OWNER just gets a clean
      403 on the Tables screen) and is precisely how the five outages listed in
      PermissionCatalogClosureTest's javadoc each reached production. Same RAISE posture as 056,
      057 and 082.
    -->
    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-083-verify-pos-tables-admin" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <comment>Fail the migration if pos.tables.admin exists but no role holds it</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            DO $$
            DECLARE holder_count INT;
            BEGIN
                SELECT count(*) INTO holder_count
                  FROM role_permissions
                 WHERE permission_code = 'pos.tables.admin';
                IF holder_count = 0 THEN
                    RAISE EXCEPTION
                      'pos.tables.admin is defined but granted to no role. TableController gates create/update/activate/deactivate on it, so the dining-table management screen would 403 for every user on every tenant including OWNER - and it would do so as a normal-looking permission denial, not an error anyone would trace back to this migration.';
                END IF;
            END $$;
        </sql>
        <rollback/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
